From bdfc6e07858ab54f98e40390c6c95a0e95bdc4e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 00:54:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Back absvectors and dicts with unnamed ETS tables

dict_new/0 created a named table DICT<n> per absvector, tuple and
dictionary, building the name with list_to_atom.  Every allocation
therefore leaked one atom for the life of the node (a few million
allocations exhaust the atom table), and it bumped the shared variable
counter, a protected table only the booting process may write, so no
other process could allocate a vector at all.

dict_new/0 now returns ets:new(dict, [set, protected]): an unnamed
table identified by its tid.  No atom is created and the counter table
is not touched.  The value shapes are unchanged, {vector, Len, Tab} and
{dict, Tab}, but Tab is now a reference instead of an atom.

Protection stays protected, as before (named tables were created with
the default): the allocating process writes, any process reads.  Public
was not chosen because it would let any process mutate another's
vectors; that is a semantic change that deserves its own decision.

Code that relied on Tab being an atom:
- shen_erl_interop:to_shen/1 guarded the boxed vector/dict pass-through
  with is_atom(Tab); it now uses is_reference(Tab).  An Erlang tuple
  {vector, 3, foo} is now converted like any other tuple.
- The dict() type is ets:tid().
Equality ('=' / vector_equal) compares tables by =:= and then element
by element, and str/1 prints the table with ~p; both work unchanged
with tids (str now shows "VECTOR: #Ref<...>[N]").

Tests: the interop suite's to_shen case built {vector, 3, tab} with an
atom; it now uses a real table and checks that the atom form is no
longer treated as a boxed vector.  New overrides-suite cases check
that 1000 each of absvectors, tuples and dicts create exactly 0 atoms
(and 3000 tables), that vector equality and printing still work, and
that a non-owner process can allocate, can read another process's
vector, and cannot write to it.

Measured with a resource probe over 1000 iterations (atoms created):
absvector 1000 -> 0, (shen.dict 8) 2000 -> 1000 (the remainder is the
per-evaluation module), redefine 5799 -> 1005, define distinct
functions 10800 -> 6000.

Remaining limit: tables are still not garbage collected.  An unnamed
table, like the named one before it, lives until its owner process
exits or it is deleted explicitly, and nothing deletes it; a long-lived
process that keeps allocating vectors still grows its table count and
memory.  This change removes the atom leak only.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01V3S3SEmxSj7HnbJJmxMhCJ
